1
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:47,560
CONVERSATION 1
It's my last day at work tomorrow.

2
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:49,840
I'll start my new job in 2 weeks.

3
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:54,120
My human resources manager wants to conduct an interview with me before I leave.

4
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:56,820
Ah, an exit interview.

5
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:58,420
Are you looking forward to it?

6
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:00,820
I'm not sure how I feel about it.

7
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:08,400
I resigned because I've been unhappy at that company for a long time, but I'm not sure if I should tell them how I really feel.

8
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:17,660
To my way of thinking, there are two main potential benefits that come from unleashing an agitated stream of truth during an exit interview.

9
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:19,780
The first is release.

10
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:28,840
Unburdening yourself of frustration, and perhaps even anger to someone who isn't a friend or close colleague can be wonderfully free.

11
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:30,000
Let me guess.

12
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:41,060
The second is that the criticism will, theoretically, help the organization I'm leaving to improve, making sure employees of the future are less likely to encounter what I did?

13
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:42,040
That's right.

14
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:47,440
But the problem with the company improvement part is that very often it doesn't happen.

15
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:51,860
An exit interview is supposed to be private, but often isn't.

16
00:01:51,860 --> 00:01:57,460
In my company, the information gained from these interviews is often not confidential.

17
00:01:57,460 --> 00:02:04,060
The information is used as dirt against another manager, or can be traded among senior managers.

18
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:08,360
Now you've got me rethinking what I'll disclose in the interview.

19
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:13,820
There is always a chance that it could affect my reputation and my ability to network in the industry.

20
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:17,540
It is a pretty small industry after all.

21
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:26,740
Anything you initially gained from the instant satisfaction of telling it like it is, you might lose down the track by injuring your future career prospects.

22
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:27,840
Right.

23
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:34,100
Perhaps I would be better getting things off my chest by going to one of those rate-your-employer websites.

24
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:34,780
You could.

25
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:37,340
And don't do the interview at all.

26
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:46,260
Exit interviews are not mandatory.

27
00:02:46,260 --> 00:03:03,800
Q1. What do we learn about the man from the conversation?

28
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:23,680
Q2. What does the woman think of the information gained from an exit interview?

29
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:44,340
Q3. Why does the man want to rethink what he will say in the coming exit interview?

30
00:03:44,340 --> 00:04:02,460
Q4. What does the man think he had better do?

31
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:06,660
CONVERSATION 2
Today, I'm talking to the renowned botanist, Jane Foster.

32
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:10,960
Thank you for inviting me to join you on the show, Henry.

33
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:17,340
Recently, Jane, you've become quite a celebrity, since the release of your latest documentary.

34
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:19,480
Can you tell us a little about it?

35
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:28,580
Well, it follows my expedition to study the vegetation indigenous to the rain forest in equatorial areas of southeast Asia.

36
00:04:28,580 --> 00:04:32,500
You certainly get to travel to some very exotic locations.

37
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:36,020
It was far from glamorous, to be honest.

38
00:04:36,020 --> 00:04:44,340
The area we visited was accessible only by canoe and the living conditions in the hut were primitive to say the least.

39
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:46,100
There was no electricity.

40
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:49,100
Our water supply was a nearby stream.

41
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:51,880
How were the weather conditions while you were there?

42
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:57,800
The weather was not conducive to work at all, since the humidity was almost unbearable.

43
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:00,960
At midday, we stayed in the hut and did nothing.

44
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:04,320
It was too humid to either work or sleep.

45
00:05:04,320 --> 00:05:06,940
How long did your team spend in the jungle?

46
00:05:06,940 --> 00:05:09,520
Originally, we planned to be there for a month.

47
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:12,960
But in the end, we stayed for only 2 weeks.

48
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:15,740
Why did you cut the expedition short?

49
00:05:15,740 --> 00:05:20,320
Halfway through the trip, we received news that a hurricane was approaching.

50
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:23,180
We had to evacuate on very short notice.

51
00:05:23,180 --> 00:05:25,540
That sounds like a fascinating anecdote.

52
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:27,660
It was frightening.

53
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:31,880
The fastest evacuation route was river rapids.

54
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:35,380
We had to navigate them carrying all of our equipment.

55
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:38,700
So overall was the journey unsuccessful?

56
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:40,040
Absolutely not.

57
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:44,140
We gathered a massive amount of data about the local plant life.

58
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:47,920
Why do you put up with such adverse conditions?

59
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:50,520
Botany is an obsession for me.

60
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:54,000
Many of the destinations I visit have stunning scenery.

61
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,120
I get to meet a variety of people from all over the world.

62
00:05:58,120 --> 00:06:00,740
So where will your next destination be?

63
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:03,080
I haven't decided yet.

64
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:05,640
Then we can leave it for another vacation.

65
00:06:05,640 --> 00:06:10,259
Thanks.

66
00:06:10,259 --> 00:06:32,200
Q5. What does the man want Jane Foster to talk about?

67
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:51,560
Q6. Why does the woman describe her experience as far from glamorous?

68
00:06:51,560 --> 00:07:09,980
Q7. Why did the woman and those who went with her end their trip halfway?

69
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:59,000
Q8. What does the woman think of the journey?

70
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,340
PASSAGE 1
Scientists often use specialized jargon terms while communicating with laymen.

71
00:08:03,340 --> 00:08:08,680
Most of them don't realize the harmful effects of this practice.

72
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:24,700
In a new study, people exposed to jargon when reading about subjects like autonomous vehicles and surgical robots later said they were less interested in science than others who read about the same topics, but without the use of specialized terms.

73
00:08:24,700 --> 00:08:30,980
They also felt less informed about science and less qualified to discuss science topics.

74
00:08:30,980 --> 00:08:38,500
It's noteworthy that it made no difference if the jargon terms were defined in the text.

75
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:47,900
Even when the terms were defined, readers still felt the same lack of engagement as readers who read jargon that wasn't explained.

76
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:56,760
The problem is that the mere presence of jargon sends a discouraging message to readers.

77
00:08:56,760 --> 00:09:02,480
Hillary Schulman, the author of the study, asserts that specialized words are a signal.

78
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:07,280
Jargon tells people that the message isn't for them.

79
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:11,580
There's an even darker side to how people react to jargon.

80
00:09:11,580 --> 00:09:20,700
In another study, researchers found that reading scientific articles containing jargon led people to doubt the actual science.

81
00:09:20,700 --> 00:09:30,160
They found the opposite, when a text is easier to read, then people are more persuaded.

82
00:09:30,160 --> 00:09:37,400
Thus, it's important to communicate clearly when talking about complex science subjects.

83
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:48,600
This is especially true with issues related to public health, like the safety of new medications and the benefits of vaccines.

84
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:54,640
Schulman concedes that the use of jargon is appropriate with scientific audiences.

85
00:09:54,640 --> 00:10:00,680
But scientists who want to communicate with the general public need to modify their language.

86
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:10,400
They need to eliminate jargon.

87
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:30,280
Q9. What does the passage say about the use of jargon terms by experts?

88
00:10:30,280 --> 00:10:50,700
Q10. What do researchers find about people reading scientific articles containing jargon terms?

89
00:10:50,700 --> 00:11:12,660
Q11. What does Schulman suggest scientists do when communicating with the general public?

90
00:11:12,660 --> 00:11:27,140
PASSAGE 2
At the beginning of the twentieth century, on the Gulf Coast in the US state of Texas, there was a hill where gas leakage was so noticeable that schoolboys would sometimes set the hill on fire.

91
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:35,380
Patio Higgins, a disreputable local businessman, became convinced that there was oil below the gassy hill.

92
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:40,500
Oil wells weren't drilled back then; they were essentially dug.

93
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:47,120
The sand under the hill defeated several attempts by Higgins' workers to make a proper hole.

94
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:52,940
Higgins had forecast oil at 1,000 feet, a totally made-up figure.

95
00:11:52,940 --> 00:11:58,380
Higgins subsequently hired a mining engineer, Captain Anthony Lucas.

96
00:11:58,380 --> 00:12:07,900
After encountering several setbacks, Captain Lucas decided to use a drill, and his innovations created the modern oil drilling industry.

97
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:26,320
In January 1901, at 1,020 feet, almost precisely the depth predicted by Higgins' wild guess, the well roared and suddenly ejected mud and six tons of drilling pipe out of the ground, terrifying those present.

98
00:12:26,320 --> 00:12:35,180
For the next nine days, until the well was capped, the well poured out more oil than all the wells in America combined.

99
00:12:35,180 --> 00:12:42,940
In those days, Texas was almost entirely rural, with no large cities and practically no industry.

100
00:12:42,940 --> 00:12:46,180
Cotton and beef were the foundation of the economy.

101
00:12:46,180 --> 00:12:48,760
Higgins' well changed that.

102
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:57,460
The boom made some prospectors millionaires, but the sudden surplus of petroleum was not entirely a blessing for Texas.

103
00:12:57,460 --> 00:13:05,500
In the 1930s, prices crashed to the point that in some parts of the country, oil was cheaper than water.

104
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:17,800
That would become a familiar pattern of the boom or bust Texas economy.

105
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:37,120
Q12. What did Texas businessman Patio Higgins believe?

106
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:56,420
Q13. What prevented Higgins' workers from digging a proper hole to get the oil?

107
00:13:56,420 --> 00:14:15,440
Q14. What does the passage say about Captain Lucas' drilling method?

108
00:14:15,440 --> 00:15:02,920
Q15. What do we learn about Texas's oil industry boom?

109
00:15:02,920 --> 00:15:06,000
RECORDING 1
Most people dislike their jobs.

110
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,960
It's an astonishing but statistical fact.

111
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:18,380
A primary cause of employee dissatisfaction, according to fresh research, is that many believe they have terrible managers.

112
00:15:18,380 --> 00:15:28,080
Few describe their managers as malicious or manipulative, though, while those types certainly exist, they are a minority.

113
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:32,680
The majority of managers seemingly just don't know any better.

114
00:15:32,680 --> 00:15:36,500
They're often emulating bad managers they've had in the past.

115
00:15:36,500 --> 00:15:41,680
It's likely they've never read a management book or attended a management course.

116
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:50,820
They might not have even reflected on what good management looks like and how it would influence their own management style.

117
00:15:50,820 --> 00:15:58,980
The researchers interviewed employees about their managers, beginning with a question about the worst manager they had ever had.

118
00:15:58,980 --> 00:16:07,820
From this, the researchers came up with four main causes of why some managers are perceived as being simply awful at their jobs.

119
00:16:07,820 --> 00:16:15,960
The first cause was company culture, which was seen by employees as enabling poor management practices.

120
00:16:15,960 --> 00:16:26,200
It was specifically stressful work environments, minimal training, and a lack of accountability that were found to be the most blameworthy.

121
00:16:26,200 --> 00:16:34,300
Often a manager's superiors can effectively encourage a manager's distasteful behavior when they fail to discipline the person's wrongdoings.

122
00:16:34,300 --> 00:16:39,620
Such workplaces are sometimes described as toxic.

123
00:16:39,620 --> 00:16:57,900
The second cause was attributed to the managers' characteristics: those deemed to be most destructive were odd people, those without drive, those who allow personal problems into the workplace, and those with an unpleasant temperament or personality in general.

124
00:16:57,900 --> 00:17:02,820
The third cause of poor management was associated with their deficiency of qualifications.

125
00:17:02,820 --> 00:17:14,880
Not so much the formal variety one obtains from a university, but the informal variety that comes from credible work experience and professional accomplishments.

126
00:17:14,880 --> 00:17:21,640
The fourth cause concerned managers who've been promoted for reasons other than potential.

127
00:17:21,640 --> 00:17:27,400
One reason in particular why these people had been promoted was that they had been around the longest.

128
00:17:27,400 --> 00:17:35,200
It wasn't their skillset or other merits that got them the job, it was their tenure.

129
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:41,780
A point worth making is that the study was based only on the perspective of employees.

130
00:17:41,780 --> 00:17:48,740
The researchers didn't ask senior leaders what they thought of their front-line managers.

131
00:17:48,740 --> 00:18:18,000
It's quite possible they're content with how the individuals they promoted are now performing, merrily ignorant of the damage they're actually causing, which might explain why, as the researchers conclude, those same middle managers are usually unaware that they are a bad manager.

132
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:39,560
Q16. What is a primary cause of employee dissatisfaction according to recent research?

133
00:18:39,560 --> 00:18:58,640
Q17. What is one of the causes for poor management practices?

134
00:18:58,640 --> 00:19:18,460
Q18. What do we learn about the study on job dissatisfaction?

135
00:19:18,460 --> 00:19:30,180
RECORDING 2
With the use of driverless vehicles seemingly inevitable, mining companies in the vast Australian desert state of Western Australia are definitely taking the lead.

136
00:19:30,180 --> 00:19:33,680
Iron ore is a key ingredient in steel-making.

137
00:19:33,680 --> 00:19:39,940
The mining companies here produce almost 300 million tons of iron ore a year.

138
00:19:39,940 --> 00:19:52,740
The 240 giant autonomous trucks in use, in the Western Australian mines, can weigh 400 tons, fully loaded, and travel at speeds of up to 60 kilometers per hour.

139
00:19:52,740 --> 00:20:02,240
They are a technological leap, transporting iron ore along routes which run for hundreds of kilometers from mines to their destinations.

140
00:20:02,240 --> 00:20:09,300
Here, when the truck arrives at its destination, staff in the operation center direct it precisely where to unload.

141
00:20:09,300 --> 00:20:17,200
Vast quantities of iron ore are then transported by autonomous trains to ocean ports.

142
00:20:17,200 --> 00:20:21,980
Advocates argue these automated vehicles will change mining forever.

143
00:20:21,980 --> 00:20:29,560
It may only be five years before the use of automation technology leads to a fully robotic mine.

144
00:20:29,560 --> 00:20:36,520
A range of factors has pushed Western Australia's desert region to the lead of this automation revolution.

145
00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:44,520
These include the huge size of the mines, the scale of equipment, and the repetitive nature of some of the work.

146
00:20:44,520 --> 00:20:53,680
Then there's the area's remoteness, at 502,000 square kilometers, it can sometimes make recruiting staff a challenge.

147
00:20:53,680 --> 00:20:58,660
Another consideration is the risks when humans interact with large machinery.

148
00:20:58,660 --> 00:21:01,860
There are also the financial imperatives.

149
00:21:01,860 --> 00:21:11,640
The ongoing push by the mining corporations to be more productive and more efficient is another powerful driver in embracing automation technology.

150
00:21:11,640 --> 00:21:16,920
The concept of a fully autonomous mine is a bit of a misleading term, however.

151
00:21:16,920 --> 00:21:25,040
This is because the more technology is put into the field, the more people are needed to deploy, maintain and improve it.

152
00:21:25,040 --> 00:21:30,100
The automation and digitization of the industry is creating a need for different jobs.

153
00:21:30,100 --> 00:21:37,600
These include data scientists and engineers in automation and artificial intelligence.

154
00:21:37,600 --> 00:21:46,020
The mining companies claim automation and robotics present opportunities to make mining more sustainable and safer.

155
00:21:46,020 --> 00:21:52,060
Employees will be offered a career that is even more fulfilling and more rewarding.

156
00:21:52,060 --> 00:22:02,280
Workers' unions have accepted the inevitability of the introduction of new technology, but they still have reservations about the rise of automation technology.

157
00:22:02,280 --> 00:22:06,860
Their main concern is the potential impact on remote communities.

158
00:22:06,860 --> 00:22:15,740
As automation spreads further, the question is how these remote communities will survive when the old jobs are eliminated.

159
00:22:15,740 --> 00:22:29,600
And this may well prove to be the most significant impact of robotic technology in many places around the world.

160
00:22:29,600 --> 00:22:49,560
Q19. What does the passage say about the mining industry in Western Australia?

161
00:22:49,560 --> 00:23:09,080
Q20. What is the impact of the digitization of the mining industry?

162
00:23:09,080 --> 00:23:29,300
Q21. What is the attitude of workers' union towards the introduction of new technology?

163
00:23:29,300 --> 00:23:38,100
RECORDING 3
According to official statistics, Thailand's annual road death rate is almost double the global average.

164
00:23:38,100 --> 00:23:45,940
Thai people know that their roads are dangerous, but they don't know this could easily be changed.

165
00:23:45,940 --> 00:23:52,720
Globally, road accidents kill more people every year than any infectious disease.

166
00:23:52,720 --> 00:24:00,720
Researchers at the Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation in America put the death toll in 2017 at 1.

167
00:24:00,720 --> 00:24:03,200
24 million.

168
00:24:03,200 --> 00:24:10,860
According to the institute, the overall number of deaths has been more or less static since the turn of the century.

169
00:24:10,860 --> 00:24:16,480
But that disguises a lot of changes in individual countries.

170
00:24:16,480 --> 00:24:22,520
In many poor countries, road accidents are killing more people than ever before.

171
00:24:22,520 --> 00:24:32,160
Those countries have swelling, young populations, a fast-growing fleet of cars and motorbikes, and a limited supply of surgeons.

172
00:24:32,160 --> 00:24:37,380
It is impossible to know for sure, because official statistics are so inadequate.

173
00:24:37,380 --> 00:24:45,580
But deaths are thought to have risen by 40% since 1990 in many low income countries.

174
00:24:45,580 --> 00:24:50,680
In many rich countries, by contrast, roads are becoming even safer.

175
00:24:50,680 --> 00:25:00,680
In Estonia and Ireland, for example, the number of deaths has fallen by about two thirds since the late 1990s.

176
00:25:00,680 --> 00:25:13,640
But the most important and intriguing changes are taking place in middle income countries, which contain most of the world's people and have some of the most dangerous roads.

177
00:25:13,640 --> 00:25:28,100
According to researchers in China and South Africa, traffic deaths have been falling since 2000, and in India since 2012, and the Philippines reached its peak four years ago.

178
00:25:28,100 --> 00:25:33,700
The question is whether Thailand can soon follow suit.

179
00:25:33,700 --> 00:25:43,280
Rob McKinney, head of the International Road Assessment Program, says that all countries tend to go through three phases.

180
00:25:43,280 --> 00:25:46,760
They begin with poor, slow roads.

181
00:25:46,760 --> 00:25:56,000
In the second phase, as they grow wealthier, they pave the roads, allowing traffic to move faster and pushing up the death rate.

182
00:25:56,000 --> 00:26:00,920
Lastly, in the third phase, countries act to make their roads safer.

183
00:26:00,920 --> 00:26:12,000
The trick, then, is to reach the third stage sooner by focusing earlier and more closely on fatal accidents.

184
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:13,320
How to do that?

185
00:26:13,320 --> 00:26:20,480
The solution lies not just in better infrastructure, but in better social incentives.

186
00:26:20,480 --> 00:26:26,160
Safe driving habits are practices which people know they should follow but often don't.

187
00:26:26,160 --> 00:26:33,420
Dangerous driving is not a fixed cultural trait as some imagine.

188
00:26:33,420 --> 00:26:46,380
People respond to incentives such as traffic laws that are actually enforced.

189
00:26:46,380 --> 00:27:06,900
Q22. What does the speaker say about traffic accidents in Thailand?

190
00:27:06,900 --> 00:27:27,180
Q23. What do we learn from an American institute's statistics regarding road deaths?

191
00:27:27,180 --> 00:27:45,280
Q24. What is said about middle income countries?

192
00:27:45,280 --> 00:28:10,135
Q25. What else could be done to reduce fatal road accidents in addition to safer roads?
